  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-Tackling GBV in Conservation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Tackling GBV in Natural Resources and Biodiversity Conservation in Stung Treng and Ratanakiri provinces project (GBV in Conservation in Cambodia) aims to embed gender-based violence (GBV) risk mitigation and promote women participation and leadershipin community-led conservation and climate resilience initiatives in Stung Treng and Ratanakiri provinces in Cambodia, ensuring that indigenous women, people with disabilities (PWDs), LGBTQ+ individuals, and other marginalized groups can safely and equitably engage in natural resource governance.

The project is funded by the Resilient, Inclusive and Sustainable Environments (RISE) Grants Challenge implemented by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) and supported by the Norwegian Agency for Development Cooperation (Norad) for a duration of eight months, from February to September 2026.

The project is implemented by Oxfam in Cambodia, Banteay Srei organization, and Culture and Environment Preservation Association (CEPA). It aims toachieve three main results: 1) Participation and leadership of women in natural resource management and climate actions is increased and strengthened, 2) Communities adopt GBV risk mitigation strategies in conservation landscapes, and 3) Communities strengthen social norms, peer solidarity, and collective engagement to prevent GBV and promote gender-equitable participation in conservation landscapes.

In this project, Oxfam in Cambodia is responsible for coordinating the overall implementation of the project, ensuring reporting and compliance requirements, conducting monitoring, evaluation, accountability and learning activities, supporting partner organizations in the implementation of their own activities, and organizing project launching and closingworkshops.</narrative>
  </description>
